## ABSTRACT

This thesis constructs a mathematical theory of genome-scale dynamics from first principles. The genome is modeled as a nonequilibrium stochastic dynamical system on a high-dimensional state space. We develop four main theoretical contributions:

**(I)** A rigorous attractor theory for gene regulatory networks (GRNs), establishing existence and Lyapunov stability conditions for fixed points of the regulatory ODE system, and proving that under generic conditions the number of stable attractors is finite.

**(II)** A nonequilibrium landscape-flux decomposition theorem for the genomic system, splitting the driving force into a gradient of a quasi-potential and a divergence-free curl flux. We derive conditions under which the gradient approximation (Waddington landscape) is valid and quantify the error when it is not.

**(III)** A dimensionality reduction theorem via geometric singular perturbation theory (GSPT), establishing that the high-dimensional regulatory dynamics admits a reduced system on a low-dimensional slow invariant manifold, and bounding the approximation error. We additionally treat the failure of normal hyperbolicity at bifurcation points via the geometric blow-up method of Jelbart et al. (2025).

**(IV)** A first-principles derivation of the Genome Stability Index (GSI) as a functional of entropy, connectivity, and stability. We derive the GSI from a variational principle on the information manifold of cellular states, rather than postulating it. We establish its monotone decrease along pathological state transitions and its scaling behavior in the large-N limit. We bound the errors introduced by the Gaussian approximation via Edgeworth expansion, propagate network inference uncertainty via the Bauer-Fike theorem and Bayesian posteriors, derive first-order nonequilibrium corrections, and state the universality conjecture with its renormalization group interpretation. The delay-differential extension is treated in Appendix B.6.

