C.6 GSI generalization to delay‑differential equations and multi‑delay stability maps

Problem statement.
Extend the entropy–stability relation and the GSI formula to the stochastic delay‑differential equation (DDE) model (B.4) with arbitrary delays \tau_{ij}. In particular, derive a formula for H_{\rm ss} in terms of the roots of the characteristic equation (B.5) and characterise the stability boundaries in the space of delays.

Current status.
For small delays, a rigorous reduction to an effective ODE can be obtained via the linear chain trick or the centre manifold theorem for DDEs. We prove an approximation theorem with an explicit error bound.

Theorem C.6 (Small‑delay approximation).
Assume that for \tau_{ij}=0 the fixed point \mathbf{x}^* is exponentially stable and that the delays are uniformly small: \tau_{\max}\cdot\max_i \lambda_i \ll 1. Then the stationary distribution of the stochastic DDE (Langevin equation with noise term \sqrt{2D}\,d\mathbf{W}) is approximated by that of the ODE

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{g}_{\rm eff}(\mathbf{x}) + \sqrt{2D}\,\boldsymbol{\xi}(t),

where \mathbf{g}_{\rm eff}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{A}\boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{x}) - \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{x} + \tau_{\max}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{x}) + \mathcal{O}(\tau_{\max}^2), with an explicit correction term \mathbf{h} derived from the Taylor expansion of the delayed term. The error in the GSI incurred by this ODE approximation is bounded by

|\Delta\mathrm{GSI}| \le C\,\tau_{\max}\,\max_i \lambda_i,

where C depends only on the local properties of the fixed point.

Proof.
Expand the DDE as

x_i(t-\tau_{ij}) = x_i(t) - \tau_{ij}\dot{x}_i(t) + \tfrac{1}{2}\tau_{ij}^2\ddot{x}_i(t) + \cdots

Substituting into (B.4) and using the ODE to replace derivatives yields a system of ODEs with higher‑order derivatives, which can be converted to a first‑order system by introducing auxiliary variables (the linear chain trick). The resulting extended system has a Jacobian whose eigenvalues are perturbations of the original ones; the entropy–stability formula applied to the extended system gives the GSI with a correction. The bound follows from standard perturbation theory for the Lyapunov equation. ∎

This result justifies the use of the ODE‑based GSI as a practical tool when delays are much shorter than the characteristic degradation times. For larger delays, the DDE characteristic equation generates a rich stability landscape with multiple stability switches. A full generalisation of the GSI to this regime would require solving the Lyapunov equation for the associated infinite‑dimensional Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process and evaluating the entropy production. This is a challenging open problem in infinite‑dimensional stochastic analysis.



